sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/metabolic-scaling-exponent--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean formalization of the WBE network derivation with the finite-size exponent" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> ;
    dct:description "Make the disputed step explicit. Model: a branching network with N levels, branching ratio n, radius ratio β and length ratio γ per level, invariant terminal units (capillaries) and total blood volume V_b ∝ M. Define V_b(N) = Σ_{k=0}^{N} n^k π r_k² l_k with r_k = β^k r₀, l_k = γ^k l₀, so V_b = π r₀² l₀ Σ (nβ²γ)^k. Theorem 1 (exact finite form): with terminal size fixed, N_cap = n^N and V_b = V_cap · ((nβ²γ)^{−(N+1)} − 1)/((nβ²γ)^{−1} − 1) for nβ²γ < 1. Theorem 2 (asymptotic exponent): under area preservation β = n^{−1/2} and space filling γ = n^{−1/3}, and V_b ∝ M, the ratio log N_cap / log M → 3/4 as N → ∞, but for finite N the effective exponent is strictly less than 3/4 with an explicit correction term, matching Savage et al. 2008's finite-size analysis. Theorem 3: if area-preserving branching holds only below a transition level and Poiseuille scaling (β = n^{−1/3}) above it, the limiting exponent is different (explicit formula). The hypotheses V_b ∝ M and invariant terminal units are exactly where Kozłowski and Konarzewski object, and they appear as named hypotheses." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A Lean module proving that WBE's 3/4 is the N → ∞ limit under four named hypotheses, with the exact finite-N exponent, compiled with only standard axioms. Each published objection then corresponds to dropping a named hypothesis." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The formalization shows that the published derivation needs a hypothesis not listed (for example a specific relation between N and M) to get 3/4, which is a substantive finding about WBE rather than a failure, or the limit proofs in Mathlib take longer than budgeted." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the network sums in Lean (Finset.sum over levels, geometric series). 2. Prove Theorem 1 by geometric-series lemmas, and Theorem 2 by limits (Filter.Tendsto) on logarithms. 3. Prove Theorem 3 for the two-regime network. 4. Write a report with each hypothesis named and linked to Thompson's ch03-07 and ch02-01 claims; state which are empirical." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mathlib" ;
    skos:note "Tool: allometry" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-01> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-10> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1000171> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/metabolic-scaling-exponent> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is there a universal metabolic scaling exponent?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.276.5309.122> dct:title "A General Model for the Origin of Allometric Scaling Laws in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "West, Brown and Enquist 1997, the derivation to formalize" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0269-8463.2004.00830.x> dct:title "Is West, Brown and Enquist's model of allometric scaling mathematically correct and biologically relevant?" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kozłowski and Konarzewski 2004, the objection to encode as a dropped hypothesis" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1000171> dct:title "Sizing Up Allometric Scaling Theory" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Savage et al. 2008, Sizing up allometric scaling theory (finite-size corrections)" .
}

